WEBVTT

00:00:00.445 --> 00:00:06.177
Journey twenty-nine: give feedback. A member rates Jubilee, reports a

00:00:06.177 --> 00:00:12.158
bug or sends kudos from any screen, answers a check-in, and follows what

00:00:12.158 --> 00:00:17.142
became of it. This run closed the last tests of the feedback

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:19.717
requirements, L2-210 to L2-214.

00:00:20.571 --> 00:00:25.410
Joanna is signed in on Browse. At the bottom right is the Feedback

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:30.322
launcher. It opens by a click, a tap, Enter or Space; new specs now

00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:35.381
prove Space and touch, and that the feedback page and the error pages

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:36.627
show no launcher.

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:41.534
The panel asks how Jubilee is working for her so far.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:46.929
Five stars, each with its word. She chooses four, Good, and the note

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:52.641
asks what's working well. Send rating, and the panel thanks her by name.

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:57.958
Report a bug asks what went wrong, and says what will be attached: the

00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:02.176
screen she is on, her browser and device, and the app's version.

00:01:02.878 --> 00:01:07.227
Kudos are chips, as many as she likes, each saying whether it is

00:01:07.227 --> 00:01:11.847
pressed. On a touch screen a chip can now be pressed over forty-four

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:15.584
pixels, while it is still drawn thirty-two pixels tall.

00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:18.982
That was the one defect this run found, and fixed.

00:01:19.684 --> 00:01:24.886
After a meeting request, the confirmation asks one question: how easy

00:01:24.886 --> 00:01:29.862
was it to send your request? A low score asks what got in the way.

00:01:29.862 --> 00:01:34.612
The question is a courtesy, never an error on a success screen.

00:01:36.414 --> 00:01:41.586
The full form is the feedback page. Rate asks about the whole of Jubilee

00:01:41.586 --> 00:01:46.470
and then each part of it, and she can skip any part she hasn't used.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:51.685
Beside the form, Your feedback lists what she sent, newest first: each

00:01:51.685 --> 00:01:56.133
with its date, its status in words, the reference of a bug report and

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:57.229
the team's reply.

00:01:58.087 --> 00:02:01.926
Behind it, the API answers only the member of the session.

00:02:01.926 --> 00:02:06.625
New tests prove that a PNG screenshot is cleaned and stored, that kudos

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:11.192
sent twice with the same key are stored once, and that the list never

00:02:11.192 --> 00:02:12.847
carries what was written.

00:02:13.705 --> 00:02:18.354
For a member who prefers reduced motion, the launcher and the panel now

00:02:18.354 --> 00:02:21.301
appear without movement, and a spec holds it.

00:02:21.301 --> 00:02:25.033
Each new spec was first seen failing against broken code.

00:02:25.891 --> 00:02:30.966
The parity specs of the six feedback components will compare with the

00:02:30.966 --> 00:02:36.188
Magic Patterns design the moment the owner saves its captures, by name,

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:39.571
with no change to a spec. ADR 0059 records it.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.606
What is left is the owner's: export the Feedback captures, and run the

00:02:45.606 --> 00:02:50.486
sweeps by hand. Journey twenty-nine stands at ninety-nine percent.
